Introduction:
-------------
Parallel Query (PQ) is a technique that leverages multi-core CPU resources
to shorten query execution times, thereby accelerating performance. Its
fundamental principle involves splitting a query task into subtasks and
distributing them across multiple CPU cores for concurrent processing.

The performance improvement from PQ is theoretically proportional
to the number of CPU cores; meaning, a higher degree of parallelism(DOP)
allows the use of more CPU cores, leading to a greater potential speedup.

AliCloud PolarDB supports PQ and currently utilizes both
single-node and multi-node parallel execution engines. Other commercial
databases like Oracle and SQL Server also support PQ. Currently,
the scenarios supported by MySQL's PQ are behind those of its
competitors.

Use PQ feature:
---------------
(1) Enable the PQ Feature
Users can enable the PQ feature by turning on the global switch
or using Hint statements.

The PQ feature can be enabled by setting the parameter
force_parallel_execute to ON, and disabled by setting it to OFF.

You can adjust parallel_default_dop based on business requirements
and instance load to change the Degree of Parallelism(DOP) for concurrent
queries. Generally, a higher DOP leads to more significant acceleration.
For example, in a specific case, execution takes about 135 seconds with
a DOP of 4, but only about 30 seconds with a DOP of 16.

The PQ feature can be enabled or disabled for PQ hint. e.g.
-- enable PQ, set parallel table to t2, set parallel degree to 8
SELECT /*+ PQ(t2, 8) */ * from t1, t2;
-- disable PQ
SELECT /*+ NO_PQ */ * from t1, t2;

(2) Check if PQ is Enabled
Supports using EXPLAIN to check if the PQ is enabled for specific query.

mysql> EXPLAIN SELECT COUNT(*) FROM lineitem;
+----+-------------+-----------+------------+-------+---------------+--------------+---------+------+---------+----------+-----------------------------------------------+
| id | select_type | table     | partitions | type  | possible_keys | key          | key_len | ref  | rows    | filtered | Extra                                         |
+----+-------------+-----------+------------+-------+---------------+--------------+---------+------+---------+----------+-----------------------------------------------+
|  1 | SIMPLE      | <gather1> | NULL       | ALL   | NULL          | NULL         | NULL    | NULL | 5958903 |   100.00 | Parallel execute (4 workers, tpch1x.lineitem) |
|  1 | SIMPLE      | lineitem  | NULL       | index | NULL          | i_l_shipdate | 3       | NULL | 5958903 |   100.00 | Using index                                   |
+----+-------------+-----------+------------+-------+---------------+--------------+---------+------+---------+----------+-----------------------------------------------+

Supports using optimizer_trace to identify reasons why PQ is not enabled. e.g.
mysql> SELECT * FROM information_schema.optimizer_trace;
{
  "not_apply_pq_plan": {
    "select#": 1,
    "chosen": false,
    "reason": "Query has unsupported scenarios including generated column, unsupported data type, unsupported function and so on."
  }
}

(3) View PQ execution-related status information
PQ_stmt_executed: The cumulative number of statements executed with the PQ feature.
PQ_threads_running: The current number of threads running with PQ.
PQ_memory_used: The current amount of memory occupied by PQ.

mysql> SHOW STATUS LIKE '%PQ%';
+--------------------+---------+
| Variable_name      | Value   |
+--------------------+---------+
| PQ_memory_refused  | 7       |
| PQ_memory_used     | 5177720 |
| PQ_stmt_executed   | 30      |
| PQ_threads_refused | 5       |
| PQ_threads_running | 4       |
+--------------------+---------+

(4) View PQ work or not by flag "PQ_executed" in slow log
# Time: 2025-12-18T13:16:29
# User@Host: root[root] @  [127.0.0.1]  Id:     8
# Query_time: 1  Lock_time: 0 Rows_sent: 1  Rows_examined: 25000 Thread_id: 8 Errno: 0 Start: 2025-12-18T13:16:28 End: 2025-12-18T13:16:29
# PQ_executed: 1
SELECT COUNT(*) FROM sbtest1;

Key Modules of PQ:
------------------
(1) Whether choose Parallel Execution
The decision to use a parallel execution plan is based on both rules and cost estimation.
Rule-based decision for PQ:
PQ will not be worked when pq_support_features_switch is disabled or the number
of rows to be scanned is less than parallel_rows_threshold.

Cost-based decision for PQ:
The decision is based on cost estimation, which evaluates whether initiating PQ
will be beneficial, taking into account the Degree of Parallelism(DOP) and the startup costs
associated with PQ.

(2) Parallel Execution Plan Generation
Parallel execution plan generation occurs after the serial execution plan is generated.
Therefore, it involves copying the serial execution plan, which concerns structures.
like JOIN, Table_ref, QEP_TAB, and Items. Among approximately 600 Items, PQ supported about 270 of them.

(3) How to divide table data
Table data is divided base on B+ tree’s non-leaf records.
A data block (partition) contains two boundaries on leaf page records.
Each partition contains nearly same number of records.

(4) Message Queue
Each worker has private message queue (MQ) to pass the partial results to leader.
Leader thread receives message in a round-robin method.
MQ is implemented with lock-free algorithm.
Sending process adopts blocking mode.
Receiving process adopts non-blocking mode.

(5) Fallback for Exception Scenarios
During the execution of a PQ statement, if an unsupported scenario is encountered,
the code logic can fall back to serial execution.
- When the optimizer decides to execute a PQ, if an error occurs before plan copying (pq_clone)
or plan refactoring (refix_field), the system directly runs the serial execution plan.
- If an error occurs (e.g., creating temporary tables, creating parallel execution plans) after plan copying (pq_clone)
and plan refactoring (refix_field), statement-level retry is performed.

Key differences compared with MySQL (based on MySQL 8.0.22)
--------------------------------------------------------------
(1) Range Optimizer Changes
MySQL 8.0.27 refactored the Range optimizer into an independent operator (Bug #32996884).
The implementation differs significantly from version 8.0.22, requiring adaptations for
PQ compatibility.
(2) New Set Operations
MySQL 8.0.31 added support for INTERSECT and EXCEPT operations (WL #13459). PQ
do not support support these new operators and add new test to cover this scenario.
(3) Cost-Based Optimizer Enhancements
MySQL 8.0.31, all operators support cost and row count estimation. The PQ's
decision-making process, which relies on cost-based decisions for selecting parallel execution,
needs to be adapted to these changes.
(4) SQL Layer Refactoring
MySQL 8.0 refactored fundamental SQL layer classes (e.g., SELECT_LEX/SELECT_LEX_UNIT) (WL#11350).
This core change requires adaptations in the PQ implementation.
(5) Plan Cache Dependency
Plan Cache feature relies on clone structures like JOIN, QEP_TAB, and Item. Since PQ
uses copying functions (pq_clone) that interact with these same structures, and they share code
with the Plan Cache, compatibility adaptations are necessary.
(6) PTRC Interaction
PQ supports PTRC (Partial Result Cache) and has interactions with it, requiring specific
adaptations for proper integration.
(7) Hash Join Support
PQ support different Hash Join strategies, including parallel execution after splitting the
hash table and shared hash table parallelism.
(8) pq_clone Optimization
Use template functions with lambda injection to preserve the integrity of functions and eliminate all macro definitions.
(9) Code Refactoring and File Organization
PQ feature code currently involves extensive invasive modifications. By extracting interfaces and classes,
and relocating the relevant code into independent files, we can reduce invasive modifications and improve readability.
(10) PQ Constraints and Limitations
PQ do not support Dstore table.
PQ do not support hypergraph optimizer, due to hypergraph optimizer do not create QEP_TABs, but PQ need clone QEP_TABs.
(11) Optimize PQ related parameters
MySQL PQ feature introduces 27 parameters. We have performed the following optimizations on these parameters:
Moved the parameters parallel_correlated_subquery, parallel_hash_join_spill_to_disk, and rds_parallel_insert_select
into the pq_support_features_switch parameter.
Deleted the unused parameters pq_group_having, rds_pq_round_or_truncate, and pq_row_cost_threshold.

